WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.988 --> 00:00:05.478
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:09.940
SPEAKER: So today, I am very excited
to have Dr. David Bates with me.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.250
Dr. Bates is the chief of
general medicine at Brigham

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.490
and Women's Hospital, where he's
also the chief quality officer.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.600
He has been the sort
of pioneer in studying

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.206
patient safety, health information
technology, and how they intersect.

00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:28.210
DAVID BATES: Quality
is important in health,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.780
because it's really at the
root of everything that we do.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.220
My first passion is safety.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.120
And safety is the first part of quality.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.335
I got interested in safety when the
results of the Harvard Medical Practice

00:00:41.335 --> 00:00:42.370
Study came out.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.810
And they showed that lots of patients
were being harmed unnecessarily.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.270
I was interested in
physician decision-making.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.190
And it became clear
to me that if we could

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:57.250
computerize drug ordering,
that we could make

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.960
a really big dent in medication safety.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.770
And medications were the
single leading cause of harm.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:06.340
The reason that I care
about safety is I think

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.110
it's awful to harm people
with the care that we deliver.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.960
And I know that we can
do better around this.

